1
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,540
You're here.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:07,380
Your organs are failing.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,300
And your death is drawing near.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,440
But if you bribe the right politicians in the right order at the right time,

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,600
you can trade the nuclear warheads for medicine.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,080
And it's not even a crime.

7
00:01:40,404 --> 00:01:43,264
The Untapped Therapeutic Frontier There are nine point five million possible

8
00:01:43,264 --> 00:01:45,284
drug-disease combinations.

9
00:01:45,784 --> 00:01:48,204
You've tested thirty-two thousand five hundred.

10
00:01:48,864 --> 00:01:52,344
That's like searching for your keys in zero point three four percent of

11
00:01:52,344 --> 00:01:54,324
your house and declaring them lost.

12
00:01:54,924 --> 00:01:59,604
The other ninety-nine point seven percent is sitting right there, untested,

13
00:02:00,024 --> 00:02:03,124
because the molecules weren't profitable enough to bother.

14
00:02:03,584 --> 00:02:07,424
How You Accidentally Cured Three Things and Then Stopped.

15
00:02:08,704 --> 00:02:13,284
In nineteen ninety-six, a team of Pfizer scientists sat in a room looking

16
00:02:13,284 --> 00:02:17,324
at clinical trial data for a chest pain drug called sildenafil.

17
00:02:18,164 --> 00:02:18,664
The drug had failed.

18
00:02:19,544 --> 00:02:20,864
It did not fix chest pain.

19
00:02:21,724 --> 00:02:22,944
Nobody's chest felt better.

20
00:02:23,724 --> 00:02:27,504
The trial was, by every metric that mattered to the people who paid for

21
00:02:27,504 --> 00:02:29,984
it, a complete waste of money.

22
00:02:31,284 --> 00:02:35,944
Then the test subjects refused to return their leftover pills.

23
00:02:36,644 --> 00:02:38,304
This had never happened before.

24
00:02:38,944 --> 00:02:40,984
People don't hoard failed medicine.

25
00:02:41,624 --> 00:02:43,114
The scientists investigated.

26
00:02:43,524 --> 00:02:47,824
What they discovered was that sildenafil had a side effect so commercially

27
00:02:47,824 --> 00:02:52,844
valuable it would generate one point nine billion dollars per year for the

28
00:02:52,844 --> 00:02:54,144
next two decades.

29
00:02:55,084 --> 00:02:56,594
You know it as Viagra.

30
00:02:57,464 --> 00:03:02,044
The cure for erectile dysfunction was hiding inside a failed heart drug,

31
00:03:02,464 --> 00:03:06,504
and the only reason anyone found it was because a group of men did something

32
00:03:06,504 --> 00:03:08,884
unusual with a feedback form.

33
00:03:09,864 --> 00:03:12,344
Minoxidil has a similar origin story.

34
00:03:13,064 --> 00:03:14,394
Developed for high blood pressure.

35
00:03:14,904 --> 00:03:15,744
Failed at that too.

36
00:03:16,344 --> 00:03:16,844
Side effect:

37
00:03:17,124 --> 00:03:17,624
hair growth.

38
00:03:18,264 --> 00:03:18,854
It became Rogaine.

39
00:03:19,984 --> 00:03:25,364
Then sildenafil got repurposed *again* for pulmonary hypertension, making

40
00:03:25,364 --> 00:03:29,664
it three cures from two failed drugs, none of them intentional.

41
00:03:30,784 --> 00:03:35,084
On Wishonia, when we heard about this, we asked the obvious question:

42
00:03:35,084 --> 00:03:38,204
"So then you tested all the other drugs for all the other side effects,

43
00:03:38,684 --> 00:03:39,184
right?".

44
00:03:39,784 --> 00:03:40,284
You did not.

45
00:03:40,944 --> 00:03:46,044
You found three cures by accident, said "huh, neat," and went back to testing

46
00:03:46,044 --> 00:03:51,023
the same twelve biological targets that pharmaceutical companies can patent.

47
00:03:51,864 --> 00:03:56,114
This is like finding buried treasure in your backyard, selling the treasure,

48
00:03:56,284 --> 00:03:59,744
and then never digging in your backyard again because the first hole was

49
00:03:59,744 --> 00:04:01,414
supposed to be for a fence post.

50
00:04:02,324 --> 00:04:05,064
I have been observing your species for eighty years.

51
00:04:05,844 --> 00:04:07,834
I have seen you do many confusing things.

52
00:04:08,304 --> 00:04:11,984
But this one keeps me up at night, which is saying something because I don't

53
00:04:11,984 --> 00:04:12,484
sleep.

54
00:04:13,804 --> 00:04:17,844
Your Medicine Cabinet (After five thousand years of Civilization).

55
00:04:19,104 --> 00:04:20,004
Prescriptions:

56
00:04:20,264 --> 00:04:22,364
twenty thousand products approved in the U.S.

57
00:04:22,964 --> 00:04:27,044
Sounds impressive until you realize most are variations of each other.

58
00:04:27,424 --> 00:04:31,544
Your pharmaceutical industry has invented three hundred ways to sell the

59
00:04:31,544 --> 00:04:34,664
same painkiller in different-colored packaging.

60
00:04:35,204 --> 00:04:38,944
On Wishonia, this would be considered a graphic design achievement, not

61
00:04:38,944 --> 00:04:39,604
a medical one.

62
00:04:40,564 --> 00:04:41,924
Unique Ingredients:

63
00:04:42,024 --> 00:04:46,424
These products contain only one thousand six hundred and fifty compounds.

64
00:04:46,784 --> 00:04:50,564
Fewer unique molecules than a French bakery uses ingredients.

65
00:04:51,304 --> 00:04:53,004
Investigational Drugs:

66
00:04:53,224 --> 00:04:56,944
seven thousand five hundred compounds have passed Phase One globally.

67
00:04:57,444 --> 00:05:00,464
These are compounds you already know won't kill people,

68
00:05:01,184 --> 00:05:03,224
sitting in filing cabinets, doing nothing.

69
00:05:04,464 --> 00:05:05,444
Safe Stuff:

70
00:05:05,464 --> 00:05:06,604
The F.D.A.

71
00:05:06,604 --> 00:05:09,564
generally recognized as safe, or G.R.A.S.

72
00:05:09,584 --> 00:05:12,364
list contains six hundred and thirty-five substances.

73
00:05:13,324 --> 00:05:15,124
Things you could eat right now without dying.

74
00:05:15,744 --> 00:05:16,244
Low bar.

75
00:05:16,564 --> 00:05:17,064
Still useful.

76
00:05:17,824 --> 00:05:21,544
Total unique compounds humanity knows are safe-ish:

77
00:05:21,724 --> 00:05:23,604
nine thousand five hundred compounds.

78
00:05:24,264 --> 00:05:24,884
That's it.

79
00:05:25,564 --> 00:05:29,584
Five millennia of civilization, and this is your entire toolkit.

80
00:05:30,484 --> 00:05:34,484
On Wishonia, we have more compounds in a single bathroom cabinet.

81
00:05:35,164 --> 00:05:41,364
Your bathroom cabinet has shampoo and expired cough syrup from twenty nineteen. Ways Your Body Breaks (The To-Do List Nobody's Doing).

82
00:05:41,364 --> 00:05:42,644
Codes:

83
00:05:42,644 --> 00:05:47,604
The I.C.D.-ten lists fourteen thousand ways your meat can malfunction.

84
00:05:48,324 --> 00:05:52,104
This is your species' most comprehensive to-do list.

85
00:05:52,264 --> 00:05:53,244
You are not doing it.

86
00:05:54,364 --> 00:05:54,864
Real Targets:

87
00:05:55,884 --> 00:06:00,784
After consolidation, one thousand trial-relevant diseases are actually worth

88
00:06:00,784 --> 00:06:01,763
testing drugs against.

89
00:06:03,104 --> 00:06:04,544
The Multiplication Problem.

90
00:06:05,604 --> 00:06:07,174
Here is where it gets uncomfortable.

91
00:06:08,784 --> 00:06:11,404
Take your nine thousand five hundred safe compounds.

92
00:06:12,084 --> 00:06:14,264
Multiply by one thousand diseases.

93
00:06:14,764 --> 00:06:18,584
This gives you the total number of things you *could* test tomorrow morning

94
00:06:18,584 --> 00:06:22,464
if you weren't busy filling out forms about whether you're allowed to test

95
00:06:22,464 --> 00:06:22,964
them.

96
00:06:23,884 --> 00:06:28,164
Nine thousand five hundred compounds times one thousand diseases equals

97
00:06:28,164 --> 00:06:32,464
nine point five million possible drug-disease combinations.

98
00:06:33,404 --> 00:06:35,164
This is not a hard calculation.

99
00:06:35,824 --> 00:06:37,174
A child could do it.

100
00:06:37,324 --> 00:06:38,264
Several children have.

101
00:06:39,144 --> 00:06:42,804
The children were then told to stop asking uncomfortable questions and go

102
00:06:42,804 --> 00:06:43,624
play outside.

103
00:06:44,844 --> 00:06:46,384
What You've Actually Tried.

104
00:06:47,424 --> 00:06:48,244
Approved uses:

105
00:06:48,884 --> 00:06:53,904
one thousand seven hundred and fifty unique approved drug-disease pairings.

106
00:06:54,064 --> 00:06:56,624
This is your entire medical canon.

107
00:06:57,384 --> 00:06:59,554
Five thousand years of civilization.

108
00:07:00,364 --> 00:07:02,104
Libraries of medical journals.

109
00:07:02,364 --> 00:07:03,184
Nobel Prizes.

110
00:07:03,544 --> 00:07:04,044
White coats.

111
00:07:04,324 --> 00:07:04,824
Stethoscopes.

112
00:07:05,504 --> 00:07:07,584
All of it produced this number.

113
00:07:08,424 --> 00:07:09,584
Repurposed uses:

114
00:07:10,024 --> 00:07:14,204
thirty percent of drugs gain at least one new indication after initial approval.

115
00:07:14,824 --> 00:07:16,664
The rest never get a second date.

116
00:07:17,784 --> 00:07:19,004
Failed trials:

117
00:07:19,104 --> 00:07:23,274
Let's be generous and say you tested ten times as many things that failed.

118
00:07:23,544 --> 00:07:27,084
You learned nothing from most of them because your system throws away negative

119
00:07:27,084 --> 00:07:31,284
results like a restaurant that only writes down the recipes that taste good.

120
00:07:32,484 --> 00:07:33,204
Total tested:

121
00:07:33,884 --> 00:07:37,684
thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships. The Number That Should Keep You Awake.

122
00:07:37,684 --> 00:07:38,904
Thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships tested out of nine point

123
00:07:38,904 --> 00:07:44,364
five million possibilities equals zero point three four two percent.

124
00:07:46,184 --> 00:07:46,824
where:

125
00:07:46,884 --> 00:07:51,544
**You have explored one-third of one percent of the things you could test

126
00:07:51,544 --> 00:07:53,604
using molecules you already

127
00:07:55,064 --> 00:07:56,764
have.** I want to sit with that for a moment.

128
00:07:57,404 --> 00:07:58,724
You have safe compounds.

129
00:07:59,204 --> 00:08:00,504
You have sick people.

130
00:08:01,144 --> 00:08:03,744
You have the ability to introduce them to each other.

131
00:08:04,384 --> 00:08:08,744
You have done this zero point three four two percent of the time.

132
00:08:09,264 --> 00:08:13,624
The other ninety-nine point six six percent of introductions have never

133
00:08:13,624 --> 00:08:14,444
been made.

134
00:08:14,864 --> 00:08:16,284
Not because they wouldn't work.

135
00:08:16,644 --> 00:08:19,424
Because the molecule didn't have a marketing budget.

136
00:08:20,444 --> 00:08:24,924
On Wishonia, if a doctor explored zero point three four two percent of treatment

137
00:08:24,924 --> 00:08:27,544
options and declared the patient incurable,

138
00:08:28,204 --> 00:08:31,374
we would gently suggest the doctor continue looking.

139
00:08:31,624 --> 00:08:35,684
On Earth, you publish a paper about the zero point three four two percent

140
00:08:35,684 --> 00:08:40,804
and apply for a grant to re-study the same zero point three four two percent

141
00:08:40,804 --> 00:08:42,064
you studied last time.

142
00:08:43,164 --> 00:08:47,144
"Maybe the Other ninety-nine point six six percent Is Useless".

143
00:08:48,124 --> 00:08:51,944
This is the objection I hear most often from humans who would prefer not

144
00:08:51,944 --> 00:08:53,304
to feel bad about this.

145
00:08:54,104 --> 00:08:58,104
Let me address it by introducing you to your own biology, which you have

146
00:08:58,104 --> 00:08:59,644
mostly not met.

147
00:09:00,284 --> 00:09:02,984
You're ignoring eighty-eight percent of your own biology.

148
00:09:03,504 --> 00:09:06,804
Mapping three hundred and fifty thousand plus clinical trials showed that

149
00:09:06,804 --> 00:09:11,064
only twelve percent of the human interactome has ever been targeted by drugs.

150
00:09:11,684 --> 00:09:13,294
You have a one hundred-room mansion.

151
00:09:13,864 --> 00:09:15,104
You live in the bathroom.

152
00:09:15,684 --> 00:09:17,884
You have decorated the bathroom very nicely.

153
00:09:18,464 --> 00:09:20,214
You have published papers about the bathroom.

154
00:09:20,824 --> 00:09:24,624
The other eighty-eight rooms are locked, and when someone asks about them,

155
00:09:24,844 --> 00:09:27,924
you say "we're focused on bathroom excellence at this time.".

156
00:09:29,004 --> 00:09:31,904
Your drugs are promiscuous and you're pretending they're not.

157
00:09:32,784 --> 00:09:35,724
F.D.A.-approved drugs bind to things you didn't intend.

158
00:09:36,084 --> 00:09:40,124
This means "side effects" are frequently "cures for something else" that

159
00:09:40,124 --> 00:09:41,424
nobody bothered to notice.

160
00:09:42,164 --> 00:09:43,124
Remember Viagra?

161
00:09:43,624 --> 00:09:44,464
That was a side effect.

162
00:09:45,404 --> 00:09:45,904
Rogaine?

163
00:09:46,444 --> 00:09:46,944
Side effect.

164
00:09:47,664 --> 00:09:51,384
Sildenafil got repurposed *again* for pulmonary hypertension:

165
00:09:52,064 --> 00:09:56,204
three cures from two drugs, none intentional.

166
00:09:56,384 --> 00:10:00,684
Your entire drug discovery strategy is "wander around and hope you trip

167
00:10:00,684 --> 00:10:01,184
over something."

168
00:10:01,744 --> 00:10:05,284
It works surprisingly often, which is less a compliment to the strategy

169
00:10:05,284 --> 00:10:08,534
than an indictment of having no strategy at all.

170
00:10:09,044 --> 00:10:11,844
Your diseases share wiring you haven't traced.

171
00:10:12,704 --> 00:10:15,163
Diseases cluster on shared biological networks.

172
00:10:15,844 --> 00:10:18,663
Arthritis and Alzheimer's share pathways.

173
00:10:18,984 --> 00:10:22,684
Lithium was prescribed for gout in the eighteen hundreds;

174
00:10:22,704 --> 00:10:26,564
decades later, it turned out to be the gold standard treatment for bipolar

175
00:10:26,564 --> 00:10:27,214
disorder.

176
00:10:27,804 --> 00:10:31,824
Nobody connected the dots because nobody was drawing lines between diseases.

177
00:10:32,624 --> 00:10:36,904
The drug that treats your grandfather's inflammation might fix your grandmother's

178
00:10:36,904 --> 00:10:38,004
neurodegeneration.

179
00:10:38,704 --> 00:10:42,224
You won't find out, because testing it would require a one hundred million

180
00:10:42,224 --> 00:10:45,724
dollar clinical trial that takes longer than Grandma has left.

181
00:10:46,804 --> 00:10:49,324
Beyond Your Cupboard (Where It Gets Embarrassing).

182
00:10:50,324 --> 00:10:53,244
Everything above only counted molecules you *already have*.

183
00:10:53,664 --> 00:10:54,164
The safe stuff.

184
00:10:54,504 --> 00:10:57,964
The things sitting in your filing cabinets right now, gathering dust and

185
00:10:57,964 --> 00:10:58,884
expiration dates.

186
00:10:59,424 --> 00:11:01,234
If you look at what's chemically possible,

187
00:11:01,964 --> 00:11:02,524
it gets worse.

188
00:11:03,524 --> 00:11:04,724
Chemical Space:

189
00:11:04,884 --> 00:11:09,644
Estimated ten to the twenty-third power to ten to the sixtieth power drug-like

190
00:11:09,644 --> 00:11:10,404
molecules.

191
00:11:10,944 --> 00:11:13,624
More candidates than atoms in your body.

192
00:11:13,724 --> 00:11:15,924
More than stars in your galaxy.

193
00:11:16,004 --> 00:11:19,864
More than the number of times your regulatory agencies have said "we need

194
00:11:19,864 --> 00:11:20,624
more data.".

195
00:11:21,724 --> 00:11:23,344
Virtual Libraries:

196
00:11:23,404 --> 00:11:27,284
ZINC twenty-two contains tens of billions of purchasable virtual molecules.

197
00:11:27,664 --> 00:11:28,944
You can order them online.

198
00:11:29,044 --> 00:11:30,764
Like shoes, but for not dying.

199
00:11:31,544 --> 00:11:32,984
You have ordered more shoes.

200
00:11:34,064 --> 00:11:34,804
Synthesized:

201
00:11:35,464 --> 00:11:39,964
You have made less than ten to the negative fortieth power of possible molecules.

202
00:11:40,704 --> 00:11:45,564
Tested divided by Possible equals approximately ten to the negative twentieth

203
00:11:45,644 --> 00:11:48,024
power to ten to the negative fiftieth power.

204
00:11:48,224 --> 00:11:52,544
These are numbers so small that the notation system itself starts to feel

205
00:11:52,544 --> 00:11:53,044
embarrassed.

206
00:11:53,924 --> 00:11:57,214
Ten to the negative fiftieth power is not a fraction.

207
00:11:57,404 --> 00:11:59,824
It's a rounding error's rounding error.

208
00:12:00,344 --> 00:12:03,374
It is the mathematical equivalent of having not started.

209
00:12:04,084 --> 00:12:05,804
Because you have not started.

210
00:12:06,704 --> 00:12:09,544
Why You Are Still Sick (A Diagnosis).

211
00:12:10,084 --> 00:12:12,104
I want to be very clear:

212
00:12:12,224 --> 00:12:15,844
you are not sick because curing disease is impossible.

213
00:12:16,384 --> 00:12:20,704
You are sick because you built a system that charges one hundred million

214
00:12:20,704 --> 00:12:26,544
dollars per introduction between a molecule and a disease, and then gave

215
00:12:26,544 --> 00:12:31,764
all the money to a different system that introduces warheads to cities.

216
00:12:32,584 --> 00:12:34,624
The warheads work fine.

217
00:12:34,764 --> 00:12:38,624
The disease introduction system has four design flaws.

218
00:12:39,204 --> 00:12:40,834
All of them are about money.

219
00:12:41,424 --> 00:12:42,864
None of them are about science.

220
00:12:43,824 --> 00:12:45,824
The ticket price is insane.

221
00:12:46,484 --> 00:12:52,124
A typical Phase Two to Three trial costs thirty to one hundred million dollars

222
00:12:52,124 --> 00:12:55,464
(median cost per patient forty-one thousand dollars).

223
00:12:56,484 --> 00:13:00,724
At that price, you only test things pharmaceutical companies can patent

224
00:13:00,724 --> 00:13:03,524
and sell at enormous markups.

225
00:13:04,044 --> 00:13:07,204
Everything else sits untested.

226
00:13:07,244 --> 00:13:12,804
Imagine a dating app where each date costs one hundred million dollars and

227
00:13:12,804 --> 00:13:15,364
only billionaires are allowed to swipe.

228
00:13:15,944 --> 00:13:18,284
That's your drug approval system.

229
00:13:18,444 --> 00:13:21,044
The molecules without money die alone.

230
00:13:21,904 --> 00:13:24,184
Everyone goes to France.

231
00:13:24,204 --> 00:13:30,424
Trials overwhelmingly test the same few biological targets due to preferential

232
00:13:30,424 --> 00:13:31,744
attachment dynamics.

233
00:13:32,704 --> 00:13:37,484
Everyone studies what everyone else studies because that's how you get grants.

234
00:13:38,104 --> 00:13:41,984
It's a herd behavior that would embarrass actual herds.

235
00:13:42,544 --> 00:13:45,584
Cows at least spread out across the field.

236
00:13:45,924 --> 00:13:50,624
Your researchers cluster around twelve targets because those are the targets

237
00:13:50,624 --> 00:13:54,964
with grant funding, and the grant funding goes to those targets because

238
00:13:54,964 --> 00:13:57,234
those are the targets with researchers.

239
00:13:58,064 --> 00:13:59,254
This is a circle.

240
00:13:59,624 --> 00:14:01,064
Not the good kind.

241
00:14:01,844 --> 00:14:04,424
Cheap molecules can't play.

242
00:14:04,604 --> 00:14:06,824
Most molecules have no patent.

243
00:14:07,064 --> 00:14:10,904
No patent means no one hundred million dollar trial.

244
00:14:10,964 --> 00:14:13,424
No trial means no approval.

245
00:14:13,664 --> 00:14:18,924
Metformin, a diabetes drug from the nineteen-fifties, shows persistent signals

246
00:14:18,924 --> 00:14:22,584
for reducing cancer risk and extending lifespan.

247
00:14:23,084 --> 00:14:24,944
It costs pennies.

248
00:14:25,004 --> 00:14:25,804
It's off-patent.

249
00:14:26,684 --> 00:14:31,944
The trial to prove it works for aging (TAME) spent years begging for funding

250
00:14:31,944 --> 00:14:37,204
because no pharmaceutical company will bankroll a drug they can't monopolize.

251
00:14:37,784 --> 00:14:42,164
The molecule that could save your life doesn't make enough money to be worth

252
00:14:42,164 --> 00:14:42,964
testing.

253
00:14:43,524 --> 00:14:44,204
You die.

254
00:14:44,604 --> 00:14:46,004
Quarterly earnings go up.

255
00:14:46,704 --> 00:14:51,294
The system is working exactly as designed, which is the problem.

256
00:14:52,144 --> 00:14:54,984
Unprofitable diseases don't exist.

257
00:14:55,604 --> 00:14:59,644
If you have a rare disease with no market, you don't get a drug.

258
00:15:00,284 --> 00:15:04,544
Your cells malfunction in an unprofitable way.

259
00:15:04,624 --> 00:15:09,464
The invisible hand of the market has weighed your life against shareholder

260
00:15:09,464 --> 00:15:12,464
returns and found you wanting.

261
00:15:12,644 --> 00:15:15,914
On Wishonia, we don't have an invisible hand.

262
00:15:16,384 --> 00:15:18,314
We have a visible calculator.

263
00:15:19,064 --> 00:15:20,344
It counts deaths.

264
00:15:20,724 --> 00:15:23,714
When the number goes up, it allocates resources.

265
00:15:24,504 --> 00:15:26,984
This is apparently "radical" on your planet.

266
00:15:28,264 --> 00:15:31,524
The Timeline (How Long Until You Finish Looking).

267
00:15:32,464 --> 00:15:37,904
At your current pace of three point three thousand trials per year, I calculated

268
00:15:37,904 --> 00:15:41,524
how long it would take to systematically test what you have.

269
00:15:42,364 --> 00:15:47,024
The results are, and I say this with genuine affection for your species,

270
00:15:47,604 --> 00:15:48,284
not great.

271
00:15:49,064 --> 00:15:52,104
Just the safe compounds you already own:

272
00:15:52,944 --> 00:15:58,324
Nine point five million combinations divided by three point three thousand

273
00:15:58,324 --> 00:16:03,764
trials per year equals two point eight eight thousand years.

274
00:16:04,584 --> 00:16:07,064
Two thousand eight hundred and eighty years.

275
00:16:07,684 --> 00:16:11,044
Using compounds *already confirmed not to kill people*.

276
00:16:11,844 --> 00:16:12,664
Sitting in cabinets.

277
00:16:13,204 --> 00:16:14,024
With labels on them.

278
00:16:14,924 --> 00:16:18,744
You won't finish until roughly the year five thousand, at which point the

279
00:16:18,824 --> 00:16:22,184
people who needed the drugs will have been dead for three thousand years,

280
00:16:22,544 --> 00:16:24,804
which somewhat reduces the clinical urgency.

281
00:16:25,744 --> 00:16:29,264
Combination therapies (which is how modern medicine actually works):

282
00:16:30,004 --> 00:16:33,924
Oncology, H.I.V., cardiology, psychiatry:

283
00:16:34,104 --> 00:16:36,544
all routinely use multi-drug cocktails.

284
00:16:37,404 --> 00:16:40,164
The real question isn't "does this one drug work?"

285
00:16:40,564 --> 00:16:41,904
but "which combinations work?"

286
00:16:42,764 --> 00:16:44,604
Testing pairwise combinations:

287
00:16:45,424 --> 00:16:50,164
Forty-five point one billion combinations divided by three thousand three

288
00:16:50,164 --> 00:16:55,404
hundred trials per year equals thirteen point seven million years.

289
00:16:56,324 --> 00:16:59,624
That's longer than *Homo sapiens* has existed as a species.

290
00:17:00,464 --> 00:17:04,084
You would need to have started testing drug combinations before your ancestors

291
00:17:04,084 --> 00:17:07,504
climbed down from the trees, and you still wouldn't be done.

292
00:17:07,944 --> 00:17:08,984
And that's only pairs.

293
00:17:09,524 --> 00:17:12,104
If you want triplets, you'll need to outlive the sun.

294
00:17:12,904 --> 00:17:14,004
The full chemical universe:

295
00:17:15,024 --> 00:17:19,364
Beyond your cupboard, the chemical space contains ten to the twenty-third

296
00:17:19,364 --> 00:17:23,444
to ten to the sixtieth power drug-like molecules.

297
00:17:24,024 --> 00:17:28,744
At current trial capacity, exploring even the synthesizable fraction would

298
00:17:28,744 --> 00:17:31,184
take longer than the universe has existed.

299
00:17:32,024 --> 00:17:35,744
The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old.

300
00:17:36,464 --> 00:17:38,184
You need more time than that.

301
00:17:38,484 --> 00:17:39,324
To test pills.

302
00:17:40,264 --> 00:17:42,414
But Won't We Run Out of Easy Discoveries?

303
00:17:43,384 --> 00:17:47,044
Some of your economists (the ones who've never looked through a microscope)

304
00:17:47,404 --> 00:17:49,724
will argue "diminishing returns."

305
00:17:49,804 --> 00:17:52,524
The more you search, the harder each new find becomes.

306
00:17:53,344 --> 00:17:56,304
This assumes you've already picked the low-hanging fruit.

307
00:17:57,144 --> 00:17:58,624
You are not in the orchard.

308
00:17:59,124 --> 00:18:03,464
You are standing outside the orchard filling out a forty-seven-page application

309
00:18:03,464 --> 00:18:05,724
to enter the orchard.

310
00:18:05,764 --> 00:18:09,504
The application requires three letters of recommendation, an institutional

311
00:18:09,504 --> 00:18:12,984
review board approval, and a fourteen-month waiting period.

312
00:18:13,644 --> 00:18:14,644
The fruit is rotting.

313
00:18:15,484 --> 00:18:16,684
You can see it rotting.

314
00:18:17,464 --> 00:18:19,904
You're on page twelve of the application.

315
00:18:20,864 --> 00:18:24,764
Diminishing returns kicks in after you've explored a meaningful fraction

316
00:18:24,784 --> 00:18:25,464
of the space.

317
00:18:26,124 --> 00:18:30,664
At less than one percent explored, every trial teaches you something new,

318
00:18:30,904 --> 00:18:33,384
improving your ability to predict what works next.

319
00:18:34,264 --> 00:18:34,764
The math:

320
00:18:35,744 --> 00:18:39,944
even with weak learning effects, diminishing returns only dominates after

321
00:18:39,944 --> 00:18:43,484
exploring roughly thirty-seven percent of therapeutic space.

322
00:18:44,044 --> 00:18:44,874
For combination therapies,

323
00:18:46,024 --> 00:18:50,583
that crossover is over five million years away at current pace (thirty-seven

324
00:18:50,583 --> 00:18:54,684
percent of forty-five point one billion combinations at three thousand three

325
00:18:54,684 --> 00:18:56,083
hundred trials per year).

326
00:18:56,684 --> 00:19:00,924
You cannot have diminishing returns when you haven't had any returns.

327
00:19:01,803 --> 00:19:03,583
See the formal analysis for the equations.

328
00:19:04,603 --> 00:19:07,284
What Happens When You Actually Try.

329
00:19:07,364 --> 00:19:11,964
The Oxford RECOVERY Trial during COVID-nineteen proved something your pharmaceutical

330
00:19:11,964 --> 00:19:14,524
industry would prefer you didn't know:

331
00:19:15,564 --> 00:19:20,024
large-scale pragmatic trials can run for roughly five hundred dollars per

332
00:19:20,024 --> 00:19:23,584
patient and deliver results in less than one hundred days.

333
00:19:24,284 --> 00:19:28,024
Five hundred dollars versus forty-one thousand dollars.

334
00:19:28,724 --> 00:19:29,924
Read those two numbers again.

335
00:19:30,284 --> 00:19:32,464
One of them is the cost of finding out if a drug works.

336
00:19:33,164 --> 00:19:36,444
The other is the cost of finding out if a drug works while also paying for

337
00:19:36,444 --> 00:19:40,424
marble lobbies, compliance departments, and a CEO's third yacht.

338
00:19:41,164 --> 00:19:42,424
The medicine is the same.

339
00:19:42,904 --> 00:19:44,184
The patients are the same.

340
00:19:44,804 --> 00:19:45,764
The yacht is new.

341
00:19:46,624 --> 00:19:51,224
With a decentralized F.D.A., trial capacity increases by twelve point three

342
00:19:51,224 --> 00:19:51,724
times:

343
00:19:52,504 --> 00:19:57,084
Nine point five million combinations divided by forty thousand seven hundred

344
00:19:57,084 --> 00:20:00,684
trials per year equals two hundred and thirty-four years.

345
00:20:01,424 --> 00:20:02,604
Still not fast.

346
00:20:02,624 --> 00:20:05,544
But at least it's a number your grandchildren might live to see the end

347
00:20:05,544 --> 00:20:10,224
of, rather than a number that requires your species to outlive its own star.

348
00:20:11,304 --> 00:20:15,404
The first few decades would capture the highest-value discoveries, the cures

349
00:20:15,404 --> 00:20:19,624
that current trial scarcity forces you to ignore while people die in the

350
00:20:19,624 --> 00:20:20,284
waiting room.

351
00:20:20,944 --> 00:20:22,964
Your decentralized F.D.A.

352
00:20:22,964 --> 00:20:27,044
builds permanent infrastructure for systematic therapeutic exploration.

353
00:20:27,624 --> 00:20:29,204
The goal isn't to "finish."

354
00:20:29,484 --> 00:20:33,124
The goal is to stop leaving cures on the table while you form subcommittees

355
00:20:33,124 --> 00:20:37,244
to discuss whether to form committees to review the proposal to consider

356
00:20:37,244 --> 00:20:37,944
looking at them.

357
00:20:39,144 --> 00:20:39,704
The Bottom Line.

358
00:20:40,664 --> 00:20:46,504
You have tested zero point three four two percent of the drug-disease combinations

359
00:20:46,504 --> 00:20:49,644
testable with compounds you already own.

360
00:20:50,304 --> 00:20:52,254
If you include the larger chemical universe:

361
00:20:53,004 --> 00:20:55,184
roughly one-hundred-quintillionth to

362
00:20:55,874 --> 00:20:57,384
one-hundred-tredecillionth of what's possible.

363
00:20:58,244 --> 00:20:59,524
The reason is not scientific.

364
00:21:00,044 --> 00:21:01,244
The reason is financial.

365
00:21:01,664 --> 00:21:04,504
Trials cost too much and the money is busy being missiles.

366
00:21:05,244 --> 00:21:10,424
A decentralized, automated pragmatic trial system is the only route to systematically

367
00:21:10,424 --> 00:21:15,464
explore the remaining ninety-nine point nine nine nine percent of medical

368
00:21:15,464 --> 00:21:15,964
space.

369
00:21:16,684 --> 00:21:17,694
The cures exist.

370
00:21:18,064 --> 00:21:19,804
The molecules are sitting in your cabinets.

371
00:21:20,284 --> 00:21:21,704
The patients are willing.

